PROGRAM_STATUS:
The City of Westminster led a campaign to promote the development of ADU and have provided a wide range information on the City's ADU webpage and through counter handouts. The City has promoted ADUs by: 
• Launching an online Portal at the end of 2024 to allow ADUs to be submitted online.  Many applicants used this online portal to submit for ADU plan checks.
* Creating a comprehensive ADU guide with step-by-step processes and guidance for property owners and developers;
•	Creating user-friendly ADU development standard matrices for single-family and multiple-family developed properties;
•	Hosting a well-attended ADU workshop that was recorded and posted on the City website for future reference;
•	Creating various handouts and pamphlets that address frequently asked questions, which are made available on the City website;
•	Adopting Ordinance No. 2572, which reduced the maximum residential development park impact fee for ADUs; and
•	Streamlining the application and review process for ADUs.
The City will continue to update documents and information associated with ADUs on an annual basis. Addionally, on December 11, 2025. the City held an ADU informational workshop for interested residents and developers. The meeting was noticed at City Hall, on the City's website, and through all City social media platforms.

PROGRAM_OBJECTIVE:
Update the City’s current
ADU Ordinance;

Prepare pre-approved ADU design templates;

Promote development of ADUs through City website and informational material;

Monitor the production and affordability of ADUs annually to ensure the City is meeting the assumption of 66 ADUs per year as outlined in this Housing Element;

Identify and implement potential incentives to encourage production of affordable ADUs;

Host an annual workshop on ADUs.

PROGRAM_STATUS:
The City has competed the following tasks:
Adopt new permit software system and online submittal portal; 
Eliminate Precise Plan for NSFD; 
Allow 3-story and increase maximum lot coverage for MFD;  
Modify parking requirements to eliminate covered parking and reduce number of required spaces for unit s inclusive of guest parking; 
Adoptd a revised fee schedule, and 
Completed Municipal Code amendments to be consistent with state law.

The final task, Objective Design Standards, will be completed by December 31, 2026.  The Objective Design Standards fall under the review of the Economic Development/Land Use Ad-Hoc Committee.
